.TH POWERPROFILE.CONF 5 "September 2026" "powerprofile VERSION" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
powerprofile.conf \- configuration of powerprofile
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I /etc/powerprofile.conf
defines the profiles that
.BR powerprofile (1)
applies. It is an INI file: a
.B [name]
line starts a profile, followed by
.B key = value
lines. A line whose first character is
.B #
or
.B ;
is a comment; comments cannot follow a value.

.PP
Two profiles are chosen automatically from the AC adapter, the ones named by
.B on_battery
and
.B on_ac
(see
.BR "GLOBAL KEYS" ).
Both must exist. Any other profile is a manual override, applied with
.BR "powerprofile \-\-profile" .

.PP
A key that neither the profile nor
.B [default]
sets leaves that setting alone, except
.B pl1
and
.BR pl2 ,
which restore the firmware default. A value the key does not accept makes
the whole file invalid, and is reported with its section and key.

.PP
As root,
.B powerprofile
refuses the file unless it is owned by root and not writable by others.

.SH DEFAULTS
The optional
.B [default]
section holds what every profile starts from. A profile takes each key it
leaves out from
.BR [default] ,
so it only has to list what differs. This is what makes switching reliable:
a setting one profile changes, such as
.B super_battery
or
.BR cooler_boost ,
is put back by the next profile that does not name it. Without a value in
.B [default]
it would stay as the last profile left it.

.PP
Any profile key can be given there except
.BR only ,
which would restrict every profile. A profile's own value replaces the
default; there is no way to unset one, so leave a setting out of
.B [default]
if some profile should not touch it.
.B [default]
is not a profile: it cannot be applied or listed.

.SH GLOBAL KEYS
Keys before the first section.
.TP
.B backlight
Device under
.IR /sys/class/backlight .
Hybrid laptops often expose several, so set it explicitly. Default: the
first one found.
.TP
.B on_battery
The profile
.B powerprofile \-\-auto
applies on battery. Default:
.BR bat .
.TP
.B on_ac
The profile
.B powerprofile \-\-auto
applies on AC power. Default:
.BR ac .
.PP
Both must name a profile in the file, and neither can be
.BR default .
Applying one of them by name with
.B \-\-profile
clears the manual override, like
.B \-\-auto
does, while any other profile becomes the override. They are profile names;
the
.B only
key takes power sources, and is not affected.

.SH PROFILE KEYS
.TP
.B epp
Energy performance preference:
.BR performance ,
.BR balance_performance ,
.B balance_power
or
.BR power .
Needs HWP (Intel Speed Shift). Without it the kernel runs intel_pstate in
passive mode, as the intel_cpufreq driver, and there is no EPP: use
.B governor
and
.B epb
instead. Applying the profile says so, and
.B powerprofile \-\-status
shows the driver in use.
.TP
.B governor
The cpufreq governor, for every CPU. Which ones exist depends on the driver:
.BR performance ,
.BR powersave ,
.BR schedutil
and others with intel_cpufreq, only performance and powersave with
intel_pstate in active mode. A governor the driver does not offer is refused
before anything is written, and the message lists what is supported. With
intel_cpufreq,
.B powersave
holds the lowest frequency.
.TP
.B epb
Energy performance bias, the older hint that works without HWP:
.BR performance ,
.BR balance\-performance ,
.BR normal ,
.B balance\-power
or
.BR power .
.TP
.B aspm
PCIe ASPM policy:
.BR default ,
.B powersave
or
.BR powersupersave .
The kernel refuses this when the firmware has ASPM disabled; see
.B dmesg | grep -i aspm .
.TP
.B turbo
.RB ( on / off )
CPU turbo.
.TP
.B max_perf
Cap on CPU performance, as a percentage from 1 to 100 of the maximum; 100
means no cap.
.TP
.BR pl1 ", " pl2
RAPL sustained and burst power limit, in watts.
.TP
.B nvidia_powerd
.RB ( on / off )
Start or stop
.BR nvidia\-powerd ,
for Dynamic Boost. It only does something where the firmware allows Dynamic
Boost; otherwise the service starts and logs that the firmware disabled it.
.TP
.B wifi_powersave
.RB ( on / off )
Wi\-Fi power saving, on every wireless interface, applied with
.BR iw (8)
and kept on the NetworkManager connection for later reconnects.
.TP
.B brightness
Screen brightness, as a percentage from 1 to 100 of the maximum. Applied
when the profile changes.
.TP
.B refresh
Display refresh rate: a number in Hz, or
.B max
for the highest at the current resolution. Applied by
.BR powerprofile\-x (1).
.TP
.B only
.BR ac " or " bat .
The profile can only be used on that power source, and stops being the
manual override when the source changes.
.TP
.B run
An extra shell command, run as root when the profile is applied.

.SH EMBEDDED CONTROLLER KEYS
Settings of an MSI laptop's embedded controller, written through the
.BR msi\-ec
kernel driver, which must be loaded. What is accepted depends on the
firmware, so these values are checked when the profile is applied, not when
the file is read. A value the firmware does not accept, or an attribute it
does not have, is reported and skipped.
.TP
.B shift_mode
CPU/GPU performance profile, commonly
.BR eco ,
.BR comfort ,
.B sport
or
.BR turbo .
.TP
.B fan_mode
Fan speed profile, commonly
.BR auto ,
.B silent
or
.BR advanced .
.TP
.B cooler_boost
.RB ( on / off )
Run the fans at maximum regardless of temperature.
.TP
.B super_battery
.RB ( on / off )
MSI's extended battery mode.
.TP
.BR charge_start ", " charge_end
Battery percentages at which charging begins and stops.
.TP
.B kbd_backlight
Keyboard backlight level, from 0 to the LED's maximum.

.PP
The values of
.B shift_mode
and
.B fan_mode
that a machine accepts are shown by
.BR "msiecctl \-\-get available_shift_modes"
and
.BR "msiecctl \-\-get available_fan_modes" .

.SH FILES
.I /etc/powerprofile.conf

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR powerprofile (1),
.BR powerprofile\-x (1),
.BR msiecctl (1)
